            </Main>
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

During the blower door test, we identified that your house has excess air leakage associated with the following locations:



** Around the attack hatch (can seal with removable caulking such as "Draft Attack")



** The old bathroom exhaust fan on the 2nd floor



** The older windows throughout the house, and the bottom of the door entering the garage



** The baseboard trim on the 2nd floor master bedroom



** The current 6" chimney for the furnace and from the wood fireplace chimney



Savings: If you proceed with a Deep Home Energy Retrofit of your house, I estimate you will easily achieve a 10% reduction in air leakage. This is estimated to save $ 196 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 6.3 GJ, and qualify for a $ 500 rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), if you complete an eligible wall insulation upgrade. You may even achieve a 20% reduction, creating further energy savings and qualifying for a $ 750 rebate.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x4 R12. Most of these walls are finished with siding, however there are 2 sections that have a brick exterior and there are some walls that adjoin/connect to the garage. You mentioned that you would like to replace all of the siding &amp; brick and increase the insulation on all walls. The new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP)  that was launched on April 1, 2019, can help you cover the cost of some additional insulation for the exterior wall renovation you are planning. I have modeled adding R15 to all of the exterior walls, including the brick walls &amp; the walls that adjoin the garage. There are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+).



Savings: By addingR15 to all of the exterior, you could potentially save $ 1,135 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 36.6 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 5,000 in rebates from the DHERP. If you choose not to renovate the brick walls or the walls adjoining the garage, the energy savings would reduce to $ 857 per year (27.6 GJ), and the rebate would reduce by 33% to $3,350.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>

The attic insulation is currently about 6" - 7" (R22) on average. I have modeled the opportunity to increase this up to a minimum 15" (R50). The attic space includes above the 2nd floor and the attic above the rear entrance into the house/garage.



Savings: By increasing all of the attic insulation from R22 to R50, this is estimated to save $ 308 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 10.0 GJ, and qualify for a $ 750 rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), if you complete an eligible wall insulation upgrade.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</ceilings>
                <exposedFloors />
                <foundation />
                <windows>

There are a few windows in the house that have previously been upgraded, however there are many yet that are original to the house and there were some that allowed a lot of air leakage. I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading all the rest of the older windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.



Savings: 

By replacing these older windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 608 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 19.6 GJ, and qualify for $ 250 per window in rebates (12 windows maximum) through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program. 



To be eligible for a windows rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</windows>
                <VentilationSystem />
                <HotWaterSystem>

Your house currently has a 175 litre electric hot water tank. We talked about an opportunity for you to consider an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92 or higher, could potentially save $ 608 per year in energy costs and qualify for up to $ 1,250 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an older oil furnace (estimated 83% efficiency). In conjunction with a Deep Home Energy Retrofit of your house (more insulation &amp; less air leakage) I have modeled an upgrade from the oil furnace to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.



Savings: By replacing your current oil furnace with a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially save $ 859 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 44.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP).



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms for DHERP, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables />
                <WaterConservation>

Your house has three toilets and one of them (in your master bathroom) is an older 13 litre model, and you should consider replacing it with a lower water consumption model. When replacing toilets, purchase a 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model. Look for a MAP flush rating capacity of a minimum 800 - 100 grams per flush !!



Savings:

By replacing a toilet with a new 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model, you could potentially save ~$50 per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>

Wood Fireplace Insert Heat



I have modeled the estimated energy savings (net of propane saved from a new high efficiency boiler &amp; the cost of wood in Fort Smith at the $100/cord "self-harvested" rate) if you were to install a new wood fireplace insert, used for about 50% of the heating requirement for the upper floors of the house. Getting that wood fireplace insert heat down into the basement is something your heating contractor can work on by using the new furnace fan to circulate the wood heat throughout the house.



I modeled an upgrade to a new EPA qualified woodstove (a fireplace insert) that has a dedicated outside combustion air pipe and an efficiency of at least 80%. Wood stoves must meet either the Canadian Standards Association (CSA) B415.1-M92 (performance testing of solid-fuel burning appliances) or the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) wood burning appliances standards (40 CFR Part 60).



Rebates &amp; Savings: By using an 80% efficient wood stove for 50% of the heating requirements of the main living area of your house, you could potentially save $ 1,930 per year in energy costs, in addition to the energy savings previously outlined for installing the 95% efficient propane furnace. Installing an eligible wood stove qualifies for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 1,000 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Hay River, LED light bulbs and fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation:

        - a 325 CFM downdraft JennAir   

        - one bathroom fan

        - a clothes dryer



You may want to consider installing an ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fan that seals well, uses little electricity &amp; operates at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (range hood, both bathroom fans and the dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 1.8 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current oil furnace and wood fireplace vent up a chimney and are susceptible to back drafting.



During my assessment of your home, I did see smoke or carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Fort Smith



The following Nov. 1, 2018 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Fort Smith:

Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.25 per kWh

Oil  - $1.17 per litre 

Propane - $0.87 per litre

Wood - $242 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $8.49 per bag, $288.46 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Ken Baigent, Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: Ken.Baigent@aea.nt.ca



Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and ation costs, and climah transportation costs, and climate change!</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
